\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction degrades as schema complexity grows. To our knowledge, we present the first controlled study isolating type confusability from type count in zero-shot LLM IE, spanning NER, RE, and event detection across four datasets. We introduce the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and two experimental paradigms: count curves (varying schema size while randomizing confusability) and BCS-controlled experiments (fixing size while varying confusability).

We identify two degradation channels: count-driven recall decline and confusability-driven type substitution. In typical random batches, confusability accounts for ${\approx}\,2.2$\,pp accuracy difference---markedly smaller than count effects ($-$49\,pp for document-level RE)---while batch-level BCS explains 22--53\% of cross-batch variance. The channels exhibit bounded interaction in RE (TOST $p = 0.003$ at $\pm 3$\,pp) but are coupled in NER where boundary and type ambiguity interact. Effects are directionally consistent across prompt designs and model scales, though Channel~2 attenuates on smaller models. Schema decomposition via oracle routing recovers substantial RE gains, but confusability-aware grouping yields no significant improvement over random.
\end{abstract}

