\documentclass[11pt]{article}

\usepackage[review]{acl}

\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage[normalem]{ulem}
\usepackage{listings}

\title{Does Type Confusability Shape Schema-Induced Degradation\\in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction?}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{abstract}

\input{introduction}

\input{related_work}

\input{experimental_setup}

\input{results}

\input{sdgd_negative}

\input{discussion}

\section*{Limitations}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Model coverage.} Primary experiments use Qwen3-32B with cross-model validation on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct. Whether patterns hold for proprietary models (GPT-4, Gemini) remains unverified. Channel~2 was not robustly detectable on NER at 8B scale, likely due to floor effects.

\item \textbf{Modest pairwise effect size.} Pairwise SCS explains 1.3--1.7\% of type confusion variance; entity frequency, prompt position, mention ambiguity, and type definition quality likely account for the remainder.

\item \textbf{Factorial interaction tests.} Within-dataset count $\times$ BCS interaction on Few-NERD did not reach significance ($p = 0.50$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). However, a FewRel factorial with TOST equivalence testing confirms bounded interaction ($p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.003$ at $\pm 3$\,pp, interaction = 0.47\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}), and cross-pool slope consistency ($p = 0.60$) corroborates stable rather than amplifying sensitivity.

\item \textbf{Decomposition and metric scope.} RE batch composition is tested on FewRel only; NER merge alternatives remain unexplored. SCS relies on embedding cosine similarity; label entropy or ontological depth may capture complementary dimensions. For Few-NERD, SCS is computed from the extraction model's own embeddings (Qwen3), creating a shared-representation dependency. MiniLM cross-check yields a conservative but directionally consistent estimate ($r = 0.172$ vs $r = 0.138$; confusion matrix correlation 0.894), suggesting the effect is not an artifact of shared representations.

\item \textbf{NER evaluation metric.} Our exact-match NER evaluation conflates boundary errors (partial span overlap) with type errors (correct span, wrong label). Relaxed matching may yield different confusability effect sizes, as boundary ambiguity may correlate with schema complexity independently of type similarity.

\item \textbf{RE format specificity.} RE's clean channel separation may reflect its closed-set classification format. FewRel combines training and validation splits for 80 types and characterizes classification accuracy rather than end-to-end extraction.

\item \textbf{Causal mechanism.} The study establishes correlational evidence with controlled stratification; mechanistic analysis at the attention or logit level is needed to confirm the causal pathway. The controlled stratification provides evidence consistent with a directional relationship but does not establish interventional causality.

\item \textbf{Data contamination.} FewRel (2018) may appear in pretraining corpora. However, train-origin relations show numerically lower accuracy than val-origin ($-8.8$\,pp, $p=0.06$, n.s.), opposite to the contamination hypothesis. Moreover, the monotonic count-dependent degradation curve (85.7\%$\to$50.4\%) cannot be explained by uniform contamination, which would elevate all conditions equally. BCS slope consistency across independent and within-pool conditions (permutation $p = 0.60$) provides a robustness check that is difficult to reconcile with non-uniform contamination, which would differentially affect specific type subsets. DocRED (2019) provides a lower-contamination-risk replication with consistent BCS--accuracy slopes ($R^2 = 0.53$). Adding train-origin ratio as a covariate leaves the BCS partial effect unchanged ($p = 0.245$ for origin; Appendix~\ref{app:split-origin}).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} Following the argument that independent replications do not require family-wise correction \citep{rothman-1990}, while acknowledging the opposing view \citep{perneger-1998}, we treat each dataset as a separate test of the same hypothesis. Within-dataset secondary analyses are reported with individual $p$-values; core claims rest on effects reaching $p < 0.001$.

\input{checklist}

\bibliography{references}

\appendix

\section{Additional Figures}
\label{sec:appendix_figures}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small

\label{tab:cross_model_bcs}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr}
\toprule
 & & \multicolumn{5}{c}{\textbf{BCS Bin}} & & \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-7}
\textbf{Task} & \textbf{Error} & \textbf{Solo} & \textbf{Low} & \textbf{Mid} & \textbf{High} & \textbf{V.High} & \textbf{$\rho$} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
NER & IntraSub & 0\% & 1.6\% & 10.6\% & 49.4\% & --- & 1.000 & ${<}0.001$ \\
RE  & IntraSub & 0\% & 4.2\% & 13.0\% & 20.3\% & 32.3\% & 0.954 & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
\midrule
NER & Missed & --- & 42.8\% & 64.8\% & 49.9\% & --- & --- & --- \\
RE  & Missed & --- & 10.4\% & 11.3\% & 13.1\% & 15.7\% & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) and missed-entity rates by BCS bin, with Spearman $\rho$ computed over $n=154$ individual batches (RE) and $n=148$ batches (NER). NER has 4 BCS bins; RE has 5.}
\label{tab:error_taxonomy}
\end{table}

\section{SDGD Full Protocol and Results}
\label{sec:appendix_sdgd}

We construct a thresholded confusability graph $G = (V, E)$ where each type is a vertex and an edge connects types $t_i, t_j$ whenever $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) > \tau$. Greedy graph coloring (\textsc{largest\_first}) assigns each type to a color such that no two adjacent---i.e., highly confusable---types share a color. Each color class forms one extraction batch, and predictions are merged via set union. We compare four modes: (i)~\textbf{single\_pass}, the undecomposed baseline; (ii)~\textbf{sdgd\_zero}, SCS-guided partitioning; (iii)~\textbf{random\_batch}, random partitioning with matched batch sizes; and (iv)~\textbf{per\_type}, the extreme case where each type is extracted in isolation.

Single-pass extraction achieves F1\,=\,0.445 (P\,=\,0.563, R\,=\,0.368). SDGD obtains F1\,=\,0.251 (P\,=\,0.210, R\,=\,0.314), a 44\% relative drop. Random batching reaches F1\,=\,0.320 (P\,=\,0.249, R\,=\,0.446), and per-type extraction yields F1\,=\,0.292 (P\,=\,0.194, R\,=\,0.586). Every decomposition mode falls below the single-pass baseline (Table~\ref{tab:sdgd_results}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lrccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{\# Batches} & \textbf{Precision} & \textbf{Recall} & \textbf{F1} \\
\midrule
single\_pass & 1 & 0.563 & 0.368 & 0.445 \\
\midrule
sdgd\_zero (v1) & 8 & 0.210 & 0.314 & 0.251 \\
random\_batch (v1) & 8 & 0.249 & 0.446 & 0.320 \\
per\_type & 66 & 0.194 & 0.586 & 0.292 \\
\midrule
sdgd\_zero (v2) & 4 & 0.324 & 0.191 & 0.241 \\
random\_batch (v2) & 8 & 0.331 & 0.276 & 0.301 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Few-NERD extraction under decomposition modes. V1 uses union merge; v2 uses confidence-gated merge with \texttt{min\_batch\_size=8} (reducing 8$\to$4 batches for sdgd\_zero). All decomposition modes produce lower F1 than the single-pass baseline. All modes use the v1 prompt.}
\label{tab:sdgd_results}
\end{table}

Decomposition boosts recall (per-type recall reaches 0.586 versus single-pass 0.368) but devastates precision (0.194 versus 0.563). When the same text is presented to the model $k$ times with different type subsets, each pass independently generates entity predictions. Because LLM outputs are not calibrated across passes, the union merge step accumulates overlapping and contradictory predictions, inflating false positives.

Our v2 merge ranks competing predictions by the LLM's self-reported confidence and retains only the highest-scoring candidate for each span. Confidence gating improves precision (sdgd\_zero: 0.210$\to$0.324; random\_batch: 0.249$\to$0.331), but at the cost of disproportionate recall loss (0.314$\to$0.191; 0.446$\to$0.276), yielding lower F1 in both modes (sdgd\_zero: 0.251$\to$0.241; random\_batch: 0.320$\to$0.301).

\section{Factorial Analysis and Separability Test}
\label{sec:appendix_factorial}

To formally test whether count and confusability effects are additive, we fit a multiple regression (accuracy $\sim$ $\log_2$\,count $+$ BCS $+$ count$\times$BCS) across 69 batches from FewRel spanning five count levels ($k \in \{4, 8, 16, 32, 40\}$). Both main effects are significant (count: $p = 0.008$; BCS: $p = 0.0002$; $R^2 = 0.52$), but the interaction term is negligible (0.47\,pp). A TOST equivalence test with a $\pm 3$\,pp margin confirms that the interaction is bounded well below practical significance ($p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.003$), supporting bounded interaction between count and confusability effects.

A separate within-dataset $2 \times 2$ factorial on FewRel (count $\in \{8, 16\}$, BCS low/high; $n = 54$) did not reach equivalence ($p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.56$), likely due to limited power from fewer count levels and narrower BCS contrast. The Few-NERD factorial (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}) similarly did not reach significance ($p = 0.50$). The regression approach provides the stronger test by leveraging the full count range.

\paragraph{Equivalence margin justification.}
We set $\pm 3$\,pp as the smallest interaction that would materially alter practitioner decisions: a 3\,pp interaction atop the 2.2\,pp confusability main effect would represent ${>}130\%$ amplification, fundamentally changing whether confusability warrants mitigation. At $\pm 2$\,pp (marginally equivalent, $p = 0.047$), interaction remains bounded below the main effect itself.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{crrl}
\toprule
\textbf{Margin (pp)} & \textbf{$t_{\text{upper}}$} & \textbf{$p_{\text{TOST}}$} & \textbf{Conclusion} \\
\midrule
$\pm 1$ & $-0.59$ & $0.278$ & Inconclusive \\
$\pm 2$ & $-1.70$ & $0.047$ & Equivalent \\
$\pm 3$ & $-2.81$ & $0.003$ & Equivalent \\
$\pm 5$ & $-5.02$ & ${<}\,0.001$ & Equivalent \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{TOST sensitivity analysis across equivalence margins. The interaction effect (0.47\,pp, 90\% CI [$-$1.0, 2.0]\,pp) is bounded below 2\,pp at marginal significance ($p = 0.047$), confirming that the $\pm 3$\,pp choice does not drive the equivalence conclusion. At $\pm 1$\,pp, equivalence is inconclusive ($p = 0.278$), meaning interaction could reach approximately half the main effect magnitude.}
\label{tab:tost_sensitivity}
\end{table}

\section{Within-Pool CI Details}
\label{sec:appendix_within_pool}

The quartile $\Delta$ for 80-pool 16-relation subsets ($-$3.92\,pp, 95\% CI [$-$8.17, $+$0.38]) crosses zero, reflecting the compressed BCS range within that pool (0.46--0.68 vs.\ 0.48--0.71 for the independent pool). Within this narrow band, the quartile contrast lacks statistical power to detect the effect that the regression identifies. The full 80-relation condition ($n = 25$) is underpowered for both the slope (CI: [$-$1.303, $+$1.695]) and quartile analyses; we include it in Table~\ref{tab:within_pool} for completeness but do not draw inferential conclusions from it.

\section{Controlling for Centrality Confounds in MAVEN Per-Type Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}

Section~\ref{sec:centrality_confound} identifies a centrality confound in MAVEN's per-type BCS--F1 correlation: both embeddings show a \emph{positive} raw correlation, opposite to the expected direction. Here we formalize this analysis by identifying specific confounders for each embedding and testing whether the BCS--F1 relationship survives partial correlation controlling for these confounders.

\paragraph{Confound identification.}
For each embedding, we computed the correlation between mean BCS and candidate confounders (type frequency, degree centrality at threshold 0.7) across MAVEN's 149 active event types. The dominant confound differs by embedding:
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{MiniLM}: BCS correlates with log type frequency ($r = 0.201$). Frequent types have more training signal and are extracted more reliably, producing the positive BCS--F1 association.
\item \textbf{Qwen3}: BCS correlates strongly with degree centrality ($r = 0.854$). In the Qwen3 embedding space, high-BCS types are those with many semantic neighbors---central, well-represented types that the model extracts well.
\end{itemize}

